# Razor Component Markup Handbook

## Purpose

This handbook defines the default standard for the HTML markup section of Blazor/Razor components.

It is intended for R&D components, internal dashboards, operator controls, custom UI widgets, and one-off reusable components.

The goal is markup that is:

* Easy to read
* Easy to review
* Easy to modify
* Accessible by default
* Bound clearly to component state
* Structured consistently
* Safe for dynamic data
* Compatible with clean CSS
* Understandable by humans and AI

This handbook should be used to “purify” Razor component markup into a predictable 90% solution.

---

# 1. Root Element Rule

Every component should have one clear root element.

Preferred:

```razor
<div class="component-name">
    ...
</div>
```

Acceptable when semantic HTML is better:

```razor
<section class="component-name" aria-labelledby="@TitleId">
    ...
</section>
```

Avoid loose sibling markup unless the component is intentionally fragment-only.

Avoid:

```razor
<h3>Title</h3>
<button>Action</button>
<div>Body</div>
```

Prefer:

```razor
<section class="status-panel" aria-labelledby="@TitleId">
    <header class="status-panel__header">
        <h3 id="@TitleId" class="status-panel__title">Status</h3>
    </header>

    <div class="status-panel__body">
        ...
    </div>
</section>
```

The root element defines the component boundary.

---

# 2. Markup Order Rule

Use a predictable internal order.

Standard component structure:

```razor
<div class="component-name">
    <header class="component-name__header">
        ...
    </header>

    <div class="component-name__body">
        ...
    </div>

    <footer class="component-name__footer">
        ...
    </footer>
</div>
```

Use only the sections the component needs.

Recommended order:

1. Root
2. Header
3. Primary content/body
4. Lists, rows, or controls
5. Status/helper text
6. Actions/footer
7. Optional templates/content slots

Avoid scattering actions, status, and body content randomly.

---

# 3. Semantic HTML Rule

Use the most meaningful native HTML element first.

Prefer:

```razor
<button type="button">Start</button>
```

Over:

```razor
<div @onclick="Start">Start</div>
```

Prefer:

```razor
<label for="@InputId">Feed rate</label>
<input id="@InputId" type="number" />
```

Over:

```razor
<div>Feed rate</div>
<input type="number" />
```

Prefer:

```razor
<ul>
    @foreach (var item in Items)
    {
        <li>@item.Name</li>
    }
</ul>
```

Over:

```razor
<div>
    @foreach (var item in Items)
    {
        <div>@item.Name</div>
    }
</div>
```

Use native elements before custom ARIA roles.

---

# 4. Class Naming Rule

Markup class names must match the component CSS naming pattern.

Use:

```razor
<div class="feed-rate-slider">
    <div class="feed-rate-slider__header">
        <span class="feed-rate-slider__label">Feed Rate</span>
        <span class="feed-rate-slider__value">@CurrentValue</span>
    </div>
</div>
```

Use this structure:

```text
component-name
component-name__element
component-name__element--state
component-name__element--variant
```

Avoid vague classes:

```razor
<div class="box">
    <div class="top">
        <span class="big-text">Value</span>
    </div>
</div>
```

Prefer role-based names:

```razor
<div class="status-card">
    <div class="status-card__header">
        <span class="status-card__value">Value</span>
    </div>
</div>
```

---

# 5. Attribute Ordering Rule

Keep attributes in a consistent order.

Recommended order:

```razor
<button
    id="@ButtonId"
    class="component-name__button"
    type="button"
    disabled="@IsDisabled"
    aria-label="@AriaLabel"
    aria-pressed="@IsPressed"
    @onclick="HandleClick">
    Save
</button>
```

Attribute order:

1. `id`
2. `class`
3. Native HTML attributes
4. Accessibility attributes
5. Blazor directive attributes
6. Event handlers
7. Attribute splatting

Example:

```razor
<input
    id="@InputId"
    class="numeric-input__field"
    type="number"
    min="@Min"
    max="@Max"
    step="@Step"
    value="@Value"
    aria-label="@AriaLabel"
    aria-describedby="@DescriptionId"
    @onchange="HandleChanged"
    @attributes="AdditionalAttributes" />
```

Consistency matters more than perfection.

---

# 6. Boolean Attribute Rule

Bind boolean attributes directly.

Preferred:

```razor
<button type="button" disabled="@IsDisabled">
    Save
</button>
```

Preferred:

```razor
<input type="checkbox" checked="@IsChecked" />
```

Avoid string-based booleans:

```razor
<button disabled="@(IsDisabled ? "disabled" : null)">
    Save
</button>
```

Use native boolean attributes when the browser already understands the behavior.

---

# 7. Conditional Rendering Rule

Use `@if` when an element should exist only under certain conditions.

Preferred:

```razor
@if (IsLoading)
{
    <div class="data-panel__loading" role="status">
        Loading...
    </div>
}
else if (Items.Count == 0)
{
    <div class="data-panel__empty">
        No data available.
    </div>
}
else
{
    <div class="data-panel__content">
        ...
    </div>
}
```

Do not render meaningless empty wrappers.

Avoid:

```razor
<div class="error">
    @(HasError ? ErrorMessage : "")
</div>
```

Prefer:

```razor
@if (HasError)
{
    <div class="component-name__error" role="alert">
        @ErrorMessage
    </div>
}
```

Render elements intentionally.

---

# 8. Visibility Rule

Use conditional rendering when the element should not exist.

Use CSS visibility classes when the element should remain in layout or preserve state.

Remove from DOM:

```razor
@if (IsOpen)
{
    <div class="dropdown__menu">
        ...
    </div>
}
```

Keep in DOM:

```razor
<div class="panel__content @(IsCollapsed ? "panel__content--hidden" : null)">
    ...
</div>
```

Use conditional rendering for:

* Optional sections
* Error messages
* Empty states
* Loading states
* Menus that do not need preserved state

Use CSS visibility for:

* Animated collapse
* Preserving input state
* Maintaining layout structure
* Avoiding expensive re-creation

---

# 9. State Class Rule

Component state should be visible in markup through explicit classes.

Preferred:

```razor
<div class="status-card @(IsActive ? "status-card--active" : "status-card--inactive")">
    ...
</div>
```

Preferred:

```razor
<button class="tab-list__tab @(SelectedTab == tab ? "tab-list__tab--selected" : null)">
    @tab.Label
</button>
```

Common state classes:

```text
--active
--inactive
--selected
--open
--closed
--disabled
--loading
--error
--warning
--success
--readonly
```

Avoid hiding important state only in C#.

The rendered markup should make the UI state obvious.

---

# 10. Event Handler Rule

Use named methods for meaningful behavior.

Preferred:

```razor
<button type="button" @onclick="HandleStartClicked">
    Start
</button>
```

Acceptable for trivial local behavior:

```razor
<button type="button" @onclick="() => IsOpen = !IsOpen">
    Toggle
</button>
```

Avoid complex inline logic:

```razor
<button @onclick="async () => { IsLoading = true; await Service.SaveAsync(Model); IsLoading = false; await OnSaved.InvokeAsync(Model); }">
    Save
</button>
```

Prefer:

```razor
<button type="button" @onclick="HandleSaveClicked">
    Save
</button>
```

Markup should describe the UI. Complex behavior belongs in the code section.

---

# 11. Event Naming Rule

Use event handler names that describe the UI event.

Preferred:

```razor
@onclick="HandleToggleClicked"
@onchange="HandleValueChanged"
@oninput="HandleSearchInput"
@onkeydown="HandleKeyDown"
@onblur="HandleInputBlurred"
@onfocus="HandleInputFocused"
```

Avoid vague names:

```razor
@onclick="Click"
@onclick="DoThing"
@onclick="Update"
@onclick="Run"
```

The handler name should explain why the event exists.

---

# 12. Prevent Default Rule

Use `preventDefault` only when the browser’s default behavior is wrong for the component.

Common examples:

```razor
<input
    class="search-box__input"
    value="@SearchText"
    @onkeydown="HandleKeyDown"
    @onkeydown:preventDefault="@ShouldPreventKeyDefault" />
```

```razor
<form class="search-form" @onsubmit="HandleSubmit" @onsubmit:preventDefault>
    ...
</form>
```

Do not add `preventDefault` by habit.

Use it only for intentional behavior.

---

# 13. Stop Propagation Rule

Use `stopPropagation` when a child action must not trigger the parent action.

Example:

```razor
<div class="row-item" @onclick="HandleRowClicked">
    <span class="row-item__label">@Item.Name</span>

    <button
        class="row-item__delete-button"
        type="button"
        @onclick="HandleDeleteClicked"
        @onclick:stopPropagation>
        Delete
    </button>
</div>
```

Use this for:

* Buttons inside clickable rows
* Nested menus
* Dropdown items inside clickable containers
* Expand/collapse controls inside selectable cards

Do not use it randomly.

---

# 14. Binding Rule

Use `@bind` for straightforward two-way input binding.

Preferred:

```razor
<input
    id="@InputId"
    class="text-input__field"
    type="text"
    @bind="CurrentValue" />
```

Use `@bind:event="oninput"` when the UI should update as the user types.

```razor
<input
    class="search-box__input"
    type="text"
    @bind="SearchText"
    @bind:event="oninput" />
```

Use explicit `value` and event handlers when change logic matters.

```razor
<input
    class="numeric-input__field"
    type="number"
    value="@CurrentValue"
    @onchange="HandleValueChanged" />
```

Use `@bind` for simple cases.

Use explicit event handlers for controlled behavior.

---

# 15. Input Markup Rule

Inputs should include identity, label, value behavior, and accessibility.

Preferred:

```razor
<div class="form-field">
    <label class="form-field__label" for="@InputId">
        @Label
    </label>

    <input
        id="@InputId"
        class="form-field__input"
        type="text"
        value="@Value"
        placeholder="@Placeholder"
        disabled="@IsDisabled"
        readonly="@IsReadOnly"
        aria-invalid="@HasError"
        aria-describedby="@DescriptionId"
        @onchange="HandleChanged" />

    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Description))
    {
        <div id="@DescriptionId" class="form-field__description">
            @Description
        </div>
    }

    @if (HasError)
    {
        <div class="form-field__error" role="alert">
            @ErrorMessage
        </div>
    }
</div>
```

Every input should have one of:

* A visible `<label>`
* An `aria-label`
* An `aria-labelledby`

Do not leave controls unnamed.

---

# 16. Button Rule

Use a real `<button>` for actions.

Preferred:

```razor
<button
    class="toolbar__button"
    type="button"
    disabled="@IsDisabled"
    @onclick="HandleClicked">
    @Text
</button>
```

Always specify `type`.

Use:

```razor
type="button"
```

For normal UI buttons.

Use:

```razor
type="submit"
```

Only inside forms when the button submits the form.

Avoid:

```razor
<div class="button" @onclick="HandleClicked">
    Click
</div>
```

A clickable thing should usually be a button.

---

# 17. Link Rule

Use `<a>` for navigation.

Use `<button>` for actions.

Navigation:

```razor
<a class="nav-link" href="@Href">
    @Text
</a>
```

Action:

```razor
<button class="action-button" type="button" @onclick="HandleAction">
    @Text
</button>
```

Avoid using links as buttons unless the result is navigation.

Avoid:

```razor
<a href="#" @onclick="HandleClicked">Save</a>
```

Prefer:

```razor
<button type="button" @onclick="HandleClicked">
    Save
</button>
```

---

# 18. Label Rule

A visible label should be connected to its control.

Preferred:

```razor
<label for="@InputId">Speed</label>
<input id="@InputId" type="number" />
```

For custom controls:

```razor
<div id="@LabelId" class="slider__label">
    Speed
</div>

<div
    class="slider__track"
    role="slider"
    tabindex="0"
    aria-labelledby="@LabelId"
    aria-valuemin="@Min"
    aria-valuemax="@Max"
    aria-valuenow="@Value">
    ...
</div>
```

Do not rely only on visual proximity.

---

# 19. ARIA Rule

Use ARIA to clarify custom UI.

Do not use ARIA to replace native HTML when native HTML exists.

Good:

```razor
<button
    type="button"
    aria-expanded="@IsOpen"
    aria-controls="@MenuId"
    @onclick="ToggleMenu">
    Options
</button>
```

Good:

```razor
<div
    id="@MenuId"
    class="dropdown__menu"
    role="menu">
    ...
</div>
```

Bad:

```razor
<div role="button" @onclick="HandleClicked">
    Save
</div>
```

Prefer:

```razor
<button type="button" @onclick="HandleClicked">
    Save
</button>
```

Use ARIA when building:

* Dropdowns
* Tabs
* Dialogs
* Custom sliders
* Menus
* Status regions
* Error messages
* Expand/collapse panels

---

# 20. Status Message Rule

Use meaningful status markup for loading, errors, warnings, and success states.

Loading:

```razor
@if (IsLoading)
{
    <div class="component-name__loading" role="status">
        Loading...
    </div>
}
```

Error:

```razor
@if (HasError)
{
    <div class="component-name__error" role="alert">
        @ErrorMessage
    </div>
}
```

Warning:

```razor
@if (HasWarning)
{
    <div class="component-name__warning" role="status">
        @WarningMessage
    </div>
}
```

Empty:

```razor
@if (!IsLoading && Items.Count == 0)
{
    <div class="component-name__empty">
        No items available.
    </div>
}
```

Do not show blank panels when the component has no data.

---

# 21. List Rendering Rule

Use semantic list markup for collections of simple items.

Preferred:

```razor
<ul class="item-list">
    @foreach (var item in Items)
    {
        <li class="item-list__item" @key="item.Id">
            @item.Name
        </li>
    }
</ul>
```

Use `@key` when rendering dynamic collections where identity matters.

Use `@key` especially when:

* Items can be inserted
* Items can be removed
* Items can be reordered
* Items contain inputs
* Items contain child components
* Items hold focus
* Items have local state

Avoid using index as identity unless the index is truly stable.

---

# 22. Table Markup Rule

Use real table elements for tabular data.

Preferred:

```razor
<table class="data-table">
    <thead class="data-table__head">
        <tr class="data-table__row">
            <th class="data-table__header-cell" scope="col">Topic</th>
            <th class="data-table__header-cell" scope="col">Payload</th>
            <th class="data-table__header-cell" scope="col">Timestamp</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>

    <tbody class="data-table__body">
        @foreach (var row in Rows)
        {
            <tr class="data-table__row" @key="row.Id">
                <td class="data-table__cell">@row.Topic</td>
                <td class="data-table__cell">@row.Payload</td>
                <td class="data-table__cell">@row.Timestamp</td>
            </tr>
        }
    </tbody>
</table>
```

Use tables for real rows and columns.

Do not build tabular data out of random `<div>` elements unless layout is not actually tabular.

---

# 23. Repeated Child Component Rule

When rendering child components in a loop, use clear parameter passing and `@key`.

Preferred:

```razor
@foreach (var scan in Scans)
{
    <ScanSummaryCard
        @key="scan.Id"
        Scan="scan"
        IsSelected="@(SelectedScanId == scan.Id)"
        OnSelected="HandleScanSelected" />
}
```

Avoid:

```razor
@foreach (var scan in Scans)
{
    <ScanSummaryCard Scan="scan" />
}
```

If identity matters, key it.

If selection matters, pass selection state explicitly.

If actions matter, pass callbacks explicitly.

---

# 24. RenderFragment Rule

Use `ChildContent` or named fragments when the parent needs to supply markup.

Simple content slot:

```razor
<div class="card">
    <div class="card__body">
        @ChildContent
    </div>
</div>
```

Named slots:

```razor
<div class="panel">
    <header class="panel__header">
        @HeaderContent
    </header>

    <div class="panel__body">
        @ChildContent
    </div>

    <footer class="panel__footer">
        @FooterContent
    </footer>
</div>
```

Use fragments for flexible layout.

Do not use fragments when a simple string or model parameter is enough.

---

# 25. Component Parameter Markup Rule

Pass parameters in a readable vertical format when there are several.

Preferred:

```razor
<FeedRateSlider
    Value="@FeedRate"
    Min="0"
    Max="100"
    Step="1"
    IsDisabled="@IsReadOnly"
    ValueChanged="HandleFeedRateChanged" />
```

Acceptable for small components:

```razor
<StatusBadge Text="Connected" Severity="Success" />
```

If a component has more than three meaningful parameters, use multiline formatting.

---

# 26. Two-Way Component Binding Rule

For component parameters that support binding, prefer `@bind-Value` when the behavior is standard.

Preferred:

```razor
<FeedRateSlider
    @bind-Value="FeedRate"
    Min="0"
    Max="100" />
```

Use explicit `Value` and `ValueChanged` when the parent must control the change logic.

```razor
<FeedRateSlider
    Value="@FeedRate"
    ValueChanged="HandleFeedRateChanged"
    Min="0"
    Max="100" />
```

Use binding for simple synchronization.

Use explicit callbacks for validation, side effects, logging, throttling, confirmation, or async work.

---

# 27. Null Handling Rule

Markup should not assume optional data exists.

Preferred:

```razor
@if (CurrentScan is not null)
{
    <ScanDetails Scan="CurrentScan" />
}
else
{
    <div class="scan-details__empty">
        Select a scan to view details.
    </div>
}
```

Avoid:

```razor
<ScanDetails Scan="CurrentScan" />
```

When `CurrentScan` can be null.

For display values:

```razor
<span class="summary-card__value">
    @(Value?.ToString() ?? "—")
</span>
```

Use a consistent fallback for missing values.

Recommended fallback:

```text
—
```

---

# 28. Text Output Rule

Render dynamic text normally unless you intentionally need raw HTML.

Preferred:

```razor
<span>@Message</span>
```

Avoid raw markup output:

```razor
@((MarkupString)Message)
```

Use raw markup only when the content is trusted and intentionally HTML.

Default assumption: dynamic strings are text, not HTML.

---

# 29. ID Rule

Use IDs only when needed.

Good uses:

* Connecting `<label>` to `<input>`
* Connecting `aria-describedby`
* Connecting `aria-controls`
* Targeting a specific element with JS interop
* Headings referenced by `aria-labelledby`

Preferred:

```razor
<label for="@InputId">Name</label>
<input id="@InputId" />
```

Avoid using IDs for styling.

Avoid:

```razor
<div id="bluePanel">
```

Prefer classes for styling.

---

# 30. Element Reference Rule

Use `@ref` only when the component needs direct element access.

Acceptable:

```razor
<input
    @ref="_inputRef"
    class="search-box__input"
    type="text" />
```

Use `@ref` for:

* Focus management
* Measuring elements
* Canvas
* JS interop
* Scrolling
* Imperative browser APIs

Do not use `@ref` when normal binding or event handling is enough.

---

# 31. Attribute Splatting Rule

Use `@attributes` for wrapper-style components that intentionally accept arbitrary attributes.

Preferred:

```razor
<button
    class="icon-button @Class"
    type="button"
    disabled="@IsDisabled"
    @onclick="OnClick"
    @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">
    @ChildContent
</button>
```

Use sparingly in components rendered many times.

Be careful that splatted attributes can override or conflict with expected attributes.

For normal one-off R&D components, explicit attributes are usually clearer.

---

# 32. Class Composition Rule

Keep dynamic class composition readable.

Preferred:

```razor
<div class="@RootClass">
    ...
</div>
```

Where `RootClass` is composed in code.

Also acceptable for simple cases:

```razor
<div class="card @(IsSelected ? "card--selected" : null)">
    ...
</div>
```

Avoid long class expressions:

```razor
<div class="card @(IsSelected ? "card--selected" : "") @(IsDisabled ? "card--disabled" : "") @(HasError ? "card--error" : "") @(IsLoading ? "card--loading" : "")">
```

If the class expression is hard to read, move it to a property.

---

# 33. Inline Style Rule

Avoid inline styles for static styling.

Avoid:

```razor
<div style="padding: 12px; background: #222; color: white;">
    ...
</div>
```

Prefer:

```razor
<div class="status-card">
    ...
</div>
```

Inline styles are acceptable for dynamic values that CSS cannot know ahead of time.

Acceptable:

```razor
<div class="progress-bar" style="--progress:@ProgressPercent%;">
    ...
</div>
```

Preferred pattern:

```razor
<div class="gauge" style="--gauge-value:@Value;">
    ...
</div>
```

Use CSS variables for dynamic styling instead of large inline style strings.

---

# 34. Loading / Empty / Error Layout Rule

Data-driven components should explicitly handle four states:

1. Loading
2. Error
3. Empty
4. Ready

Standard pattern:

```razor
<div class="data-view">
    @if (IsLoading)
    {
        <div class="data-view__loading" role="status">
            Loading...
        </div>
    }
    else if (HasError)
    {
        <div class="data-view__error" role="alert">
            @ErrorMessage
        </div>
    }
    else if (Items.Count == 0)
    {
        <div class="data-view__empty">
            No data available.
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="data-view__content">
            ...
        </div>
    }
</div>
```

Do not let the UI silently fail with a blank body.

---

# 35. Dropdown Markup Rule

Dropdowns should expose open state and connect the button to the menu.

Preferred:

```razor
<div class="dropdown @(IsOpen ? "dropdown--open" : null)">
    <button
        class="dropdown__button"
        type="button"
        aria-expanded="@IsOpen"
        aria-controls="@MenuId"
        @onclick="ToggleOpen">
        @SelectedText
    </button>

    @if (IsOpen)
    {
        <div
            id="@MenuId"
            class="dropdown__menu"
            role="listbox">
            @foreach (var option in Options)
            {
                <button
                    class="dropdown__option @(Equals(option, SelectedOption) ? "dropdown__option--selected" : null)"
                    type="button"
                    role="option"
                    aria-selected="@Equals(option, SelectedOption)"
                    @key="option"
                    @onclick="() => SelectOption(option)">
                    @option.Label
                </button>
            }
        </div>
    }
</div>
```

The trigger should know whether the menu is expanded.

The menu should have an ID when referenced by `aria-controls`.

Options should expose selected state.

---

# 36. Tabs Markup Rule

Tabs should separate tab buttons from tab panels.

Preferred:

```razor
<div class="tabs">
    <div class="tabs__list" role="tablist" aria-label="@AriaLabel">
        @foreach (var tab in Tabs)
        {
            var isSelected = SelectedTabId == tab.Id;

            <button
                class="tabs__tab @(isSelected ? "tabs__tab--selected" : null)"
                type="button"
                role="tab"
                aria-selected="@isSelected"
                aria-controls="@tab.PanelId"
                id="@tab.TabId"
                @key="tab.Id"
                @onclick="() => SelectTab(tab.Id)">
                @tab.Label
            </button>
        }
    </div>

    @if (SelectedTab is not null)
    {
        <div
            id="@SelectedTab.PanelId"
            class="tabs__panel"
            role="tabpanel"
            aria-labelledby="@SelectedTab.TabId">
            ...
        </div>
    }
</div>
```

The selected tab should be visible in both class and ARIA state.

---

# 37. Modal / Dialog Markup Rule

Dialogs should have a clear title, body, actions, and close control.

Preferred:

```razor
@if (IsOpen)
{
    <div class="dialog-backdrop">
        <section
            class="dialog"
            role="dialog"
            aria-modal="true"
            aria-labelledby="@TitleId"
            aria-describedby="@DescriptionId">
            <header class="dialog__header">
                <h2 id="@TitleId" class="dialog__title">
                    @Title
                </h2>

                <button
                    class="dialog__close-button"
                    type="button"
                    aria-label="Close dialog"
                    @onclick="Close">
                    ×
                </button>
            </header>

            <div id="@DescriptionId" class="dialog__body">
                @ChildContent
            </div>

            <footer class="dialog__footer">
                @FooterContent
            </footer>
        </section>
    </div>
}
```

Dialogs need:

* A title
* A close action
* A clear body region
* A modal/non-modal decision
* Focus handling in code when required

---

# 38. Card Markup Rule

Cards should have predictable regions.

Preferred:

```razor
<article class="metric-card">
    <header class="metric-card__header">
        <h3 class="metric-card__title">@Title</h3>
    </header>

    <div class="metric-card__body">
        <span class="metric-card__value">@Value</span>
        <span class="metric-card__unit">@Unit</span>
    </div>

    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Description))
    {
        <footer class="metric-card__footer">
            <span class="metric-card__description">@Description</span>
        </footer>
    }
</article>
```

Use cards for grouped information.

Avoid turning every visual box into a card if it has no meaningful grouping.

---

# 39. Toolbar Markup Rule

Toolbars should group related actions.

Preferred:

```razor
<div class="toolbar" role="toolbar" aria-label="@AriaLabel">
    <button
        class="toolbar__button"
        type="button"
        disabled="@IsStartDisabled"
        @onclick="HandleStart">
        Start
    </button>

    <button
        class="toolbar__button"
        type="button"
        disabled="@IsStopDisabled"
        @onclick="HandleStop">
        Stop
    </button>
</div>
```

Use clear button text or `aria-label` for icon-only buttons.

---

# 40. Icon Button Rule

Icon-only buttons must have an accessible name.

Preferred:

```razor
<button
    class="icon-button"
    type="button"
    aria-label="Refresh data"
    @onclick="Refresh">
    <span class="icon-button__icon" aria-hidden="true">
        ↻
    </span>
</button>
```

Do not rely on the icon itself as the accessible name.

Avoid:

```razor
<button @onclick="Refresh">
    ↻
</button>
```

---

# 41. Image Rule

Images need meaningful alt text unless decorative.

Meaningful image:

```razor
<img
    class="profile-card__image"
    src="@ImageUrl"
    alt="@ImageAlt" />
```

Decorative image:

```razor
<img
    class="decorative-divider"
    src="@DividerUrl"
    alt=""
    aria-hidden="true" />
```

Do not leave `alt` unspecified.

---

# 42. Canvas Rule

Canvas elements should have a surrounding semantic structure.

Preferred:

```razor
<section class="heatmap" aria-labelledby="@TitleId">
    <header class="heatmap__header">
        <h3 id="@TitleId" class="heatmap__title">@Title</h3>
    </header>

    <div class="heatmap__viewport">
        <canvas
            @ref="_canvasRef"
            class="heatmap__canvas"
            aria-label="@CanvasDescription">
        </canvas>
    </div>
</section>
```

Canvas-based components should still expose a meaningful label or surrounding description.

---

# 43. Form Markup Rule

Forms should use real form structure when collecting related inputs.

Preferred:

```razor
<form class="settings-form" @onsubmit="HandleSubmit" @onsubmit:preventDefault>
    <div class="settings-form__fields">
        ...
    </div>

    <div class="settings-form__actions">
        <button type="submit" class="settings-form__submit-button">
            Save
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="settings-form__cancel-button" @onclick="Cancel">
            Cancel
        </button>
    </div>
</form>
```

Use `type="submit"` only for the intended submit button.

Use `type="button"` for all other buttons inside forms.

---

# 44. Validation Markup Rule

Validation messages should be close to the field they describe.

Preferred:

```razor
<div class="form-field">
    <label for="@InputId" class="form-field__label">
        @Label
    </label>

    <input
        id="@InputId"
        class="form-field__input"
        type="text"
        aria-invalid="@HasError"
        aria-describedby="@ErrorId" />

    @if (HasError)
    {
        <div id="@ErrorId" class="form-field__error" role="alert">
            @ErrorMessage
        </div>
    }
</div>
```

Do not show detached validation messages with no connection to the input.

---

# 45. Readonly vs Disabled Rule

Use `readonly` when the user can view/copy/focus the value but not edit it.

Use `disabled` when the control is unavailable.

Readonly:

```razor
<input value="@Value" readonly />
```

Disabled:

```razor
<input value="@Value" disabled />
```

For custom components, expose the state in both class and attribute when possible.

```razor
<div class="control @(IsDisabled ? "control--disabled" : null)">
    <button type="button" disabled="@IsDisabled">
        Apply
    </button>
</div>
```

---

# 46. Data Display Rule

Dynamic values should have stable markup and predictable fallbacks.

Preferred:

```razor
<div class="metric-row">
    <span class="metric-row__label">Average</span>
    <span class="metric-row__value">@(Average?.ToString("F2") ?? "—")</span>
</div>
```

For units:

```razor
<span class="metric-row__value">@Value</span>
<span class="metric-row__unit">mm</span>
```

Do not bury units inside ambiguous strings when separate markup would be clearer.

---

# 47. Timestamp Rule

Timestamps should be rendered consistently.

Preferred:

```razor
<time class="event-row__timestamp" datetime="@Timestamp.ToString("O")">
    @Timestamp.ToString("HH:mm:ss.fff")
</time>
```

Use `<time>` when displaying dates or times.

---

# 48. Placeholder Rule

Use placeholder text as a hint, not as the only label.

Bad:

```razor
<input placeholder="Feed rate" />
```

Good:

```razor
<label for="@InputId">Feed rate</label>
<input id="@InputId" placeholder="Enter feed rate" />
```

A placeholder is not a replacement for a label.

---

# 49. Markup Logic Rule

Keep markup expressions simple.

Good:

```razor
@if (IsConnected)
{
    <StatusBadge Text="Connected" Severity="Success" />
}
```

Good:

```razor
<span>@DisplayValue</span>
```

Avoid:

```razor
<span>@(Items.Where(x => x.IsActive).OrderBy(x => x.Name).Select(x => x.Name).FirstOrDefault() ?? "None")</span>
```

Move complex calculations into properties or methods.

The markup should show structure, not perform heavy logic.

---

# 50. Loop Logic Rule

Do not do expensive transformations directly inside markup loops.

Avoid:

```razor
@foreach (var item in Items.Where(x => x.IsVisible).OrderBy(x => x.Name))
{
    <div>@item.Name</div>
}
```

Prefer:

```razor
@foreach (var item in VisibleItems)
{
    <div @key="item.Id">@item.Name</div>
}
```

Prepare view-ready data in code.

Render view-ready data in markup.

---

# 51. Async Markup Rule

Do not call async methods directly from markup rendering.

Avoid:

```razor
<div>@(await GetValueAsync())</div>
```

Prefer:

```razor
@if (IsLoading)
{
    <div role="status">Loading...</div>
}
else
{
    <div>@Value</div>
}
```

Async work belongs in lifecycle methods or event handlers, not inline markup rendering.

---

# 52. Child Component Rule

Use child components when markup becomes a repeated meaningful unit.

Good candidate:

```razor
@foreach (var item in Items)
{
    <MetricCard
        @key="item.Id"
        Title="@item.Title"
        Value="@item.Value"
        Unit="@item.Unit" />
}
```

Keep inline markup when the structure is small and local.

Do not create a child component for every tiny `<span>`.

Use child components when the unit has:

* Its own state
* Its own behavior
* Repeated markup
* A meaningful name
* A clear boundary
* A strong chance of reuse

---

# 53. Page-Level Markup Rule

Pages should be organized into visible sections.

Preferred:

```razor
<main class="dashboard-page">
    <header class="dashboard-page__header">
        <h1 class="dashboard-page__title">Operator Dashboard</h1>
    </header>

    <section class="dashboard-page__section" aria-labelledby="@OverviewTitleId">
        <h2 id="@OverviewTitleId" class="dashboard-page__section-title">
            Overview
        </h2>

        ...
    </section>

    <section class="dashboard-page__section" aria-labelledby="@TelemetryTitleId">
        <h2 id="@TelemetryTitleId" class="dashboard-page__section-title">
            Telemetry
        </h2>

        ...
    </section>
</main>
```

Pages should not be flat piles of components.

Use sections to communicate structure.

---

# 54. Partial / Fragment Markup Rule

Use helper render fragments sparingly.

Acceptable:

```razor
@RowTemplate(item)
```

Avoid splitting markup into too many local fragments when a child component would be clearer.

Use local render fragments when:

* The markup is small
* It is only used in this component
* It improves readability
* It does not hide important structure

Use child components when the markup has its own identity.

---

# 55. MudBlazor / Third-Party Component Rule

When using third-party components, keep parameter layout clean and explicit.

Preferred:

```razor
<MudButton
    Class="toolbar__button"
    Variant="Variant.Filled"
    Color="Color.Primary"
    Disabled="@IsDisabled"
    OnClick="HandleClicked">
    Save
</MudButton>
```

Do not hide important state by relying only on visual variants.

Preferred:

```razor
<MudButton
    Class="toolbar__button @(IsSelected ? "toolbar__button--selected" : null)"
    Variant="Variant.Filled"
    Color="@(IsSelected ? Color.Primary : Color.Default)"
    OnClick="HandleClicked">
    @Text
</MudButton>
```

Even with component libraries, markup should clearly show:

* State
* Value
* Event
* Disabled behavior
* Content
* Accessibility label if needed

---

# 56. Industrial Dashboard Rule

For dashboard/operator UI, every control should answer three questions from markup alone:

1. What is it?
2. What state is it in?
3. What does it do?

Example:

```razor
<button
    class="machine-control__button machine-control__button--start"
    type="button"
    disabled="@IsStartDisabled"
    aria-label="Start scan"
    @onclick="HandleStartScanClicked">
    Start Scan
</button>
```

Bad:

```razor
<div class="green" @onclick="Run">
    Go
</div>
```

Operator-facing markup should be explicit, boring, and safe.

---

# 57. Canonical Component Markup Template

Use this as the default structure for most components.

```razor
<section class="component-name @(IsDisabled ? "component-name--disabled" : null)" aria-labelledby="@TitleId">
    <header class="component-name__header">
        <div class="component-name__heading">
            <h3 id="@TitleId" class="component-name__title">
                @Title
            </h3>

            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Description))
            {
                <p class="component-name__description">
                    @Description
                </p>
            }
        </div>

        @if (HeaderActions is not null)
        {
            <div class="component-name__header-actions">
                @HeaderActions
            </div>
        }
    </header>

    <div class="component-name__body">
        @if (IsLoading)
        {
            <div class="component-name__loading" role="status">
                Loading...
            </div>
        }
        else if (HasError)
        {
            <div class="component-name__error" role="alert">
                @ErrorMessage
            </div>
        }
        else if (IsEmpty)
        {
            <div class="component-name__empty">
                @EmptyText
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            @ChildContent
        }
    </div>

    @if (FooterContent is not null)
    {
        <footer class="component-name__footer">
            @FooterContent
        </footer>
    }
</section>
```

Use only the parts that are relevant.

Do not force this entire shape onto tiny components.

---

# 58. Canonical Control Markup Template

Use this for custom inputs, sliders, toggles, selectors, and numeric controls.

```razor
<div class="control-name @(IsDisabled ? "control-name--disabled" : null)">
    <div class="control-name__header">
        <label id="@LabelId" class="control-name__label" for="@InputId">
            @Label
        </label>

        @if (ShowValue)
        {
            <span class="control-name__value">
                @DisplayValue
            </span>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="control-name__body">
        <input
            id="@InputId"
            class="control-name__input"
            type="@InputType"
            value="@Value"
            min="@Min"
            max="@Max"
            step="@Step"
            disabled="@IsDisabled"
            readonly="@IsReadOnly"
            aria-labelledby="@LabelId"
            aria-describedby="@DescriptionId"
            aria-invalid="@HasError"
            @onchange="HandleValueChanged" />
    </div>

    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Description))
    {
        <div id="@DescriptionId" class="control-name__description">
            @Description
        </div>
    }

    @if (HasError)
    {
        <div class="control-name__error" role="alert">
            @ErrorMessage
        </div>
    }
</div>
```

A control should expose:

* Label
* Current value
* Disabled/readonly state
* Description
* Error state
* Change handler
* Accessibility relationship

---

